description.narrative:
The Women and Peace project was designed to amplify women's voices in the national dialogue on peace. Although the project had only been implemented for a year, there were already signs that it could have positive results.

This consortium, composed of FDC, IMD and ACORD, faced major challenges in terms of internal control, which resulted in the misuse of funds and meant that contributions had to be stopped. For better closure management, a phasing-out plan was drawn up so that there would be no negative consequences for the project.

During this short implementation period, the Women and Peace movement, which had its first initiative in 2018, was consolidated. This movement, which was supported by the Project, created a national platform that aimed to facilitate dialogue between women from different social spheres across the country. In terms of results, this project contributed to the following outcomes:

1. Creation and institutionalisation of district and provincial Peace Centres as spaces for community dialogue;

2. More than 1,500 peace mediations carried out in communities;

3. Development of two conflict mediation manuals;

4. Economic empowerment initiatives focused on rotating credit savings;

5. Overall, the revitalisation of the Peace Centres resulted in the involvement of 8,600 women in 370 Peace Centres in Niassa, Cabo Delgado, Sofala and Tete.

These were the initial results, as the first year was the inception of the project, during which capacity building and the baseline for project implementation were established.
